# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "On the curve you measured, $P_1$ first reaches its maximum at the $\\pi$ pulse ($\\theta = \\pi$). Now suppose a device is miscalibrated, so that a *nominal* angle $\\theta$ on the x-axis actually produces a rotation of $1.05\\,\\theta$.\n"
msgstr "Pada kurva yang kamu ukur, $P_1$ pertama kali mencapai maksimum pada pulsa $\\pi$ ($\\theta = \\pi$). Sekarang misalkan sebuah perangkat salah kalibrasi, sehingga sudut *nominal* $\\theta$ pada sumbu-x sebenarnya menghasilkan rotasi sebesar $1.05\\,\\theta$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**(a)** At what nominal angle would the first maximum of $P_1$ now appear?\n"
"**(b)** Would applying a single $\\pi$ pulse be a sensitive way to detect this 5% error?\n"
msgstr ""
"**(a)** Pada sudut nominal berapa maksimum pertama $P_1$ sekarang muncul?\n"
"**(b)** Apakah satu pulsa $\\pi$ saja cara yang sensitif untuk mendeteksi error 5% ini?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Jawaban\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**(a)** The maximum appears where the *actual* rotation equals $\\pi$, that is, at the nominal angle $\\theta = \\pi/1.05 \\approx 0.95\\pi$ &mdash; a small leftward shift of the peak.\n"
msgstr "**(a)** Maksimum muncul ketika rotasi *sebenarnya* sama dengan $\\pi$, yaitu pada sudut nominal $\\theta = \\pi/1.05 \\approx 0.95\\pi$ &mdash; puncak sedikit bergeser ke kiri.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**(b)** No. A nominal $\\pi$ pulse produces an actual rotation of $1.05\\pi$, giving $P_1 = \\sin^2(1.05\\pi/2) \\approx 0.994$ instead of $1$ &mdash; a change too small to see against measurement noise. This is why, to *calibrate* precisely, NMR spectroscopists amplify the error by applying the rotation many times (for example the $360^\\circ$ method, or a long pulse train), so a small per-pulse error accumulates into a large, measurable deviation. We return to this idea in the challenge problems.\n"
msgstr "**(b)** Tidak. Pulsa $\\pi$ nominal menghasilkan rotasi sebenarnya sebesar $1.05\\pi$, sehingga $P_1 = \\sin^2(1.05\\pi/2) \\approx 0.994$ alih-alih $1$ &mdash; perubahan yang terlalu kecil untuk terlihat di tengah noise pengukuran. Inilah sebabnya, untuk *mengkalibrasi* dengan presisi, para ahli spektroskopi NMR memperkuat galatnya dengan menerapkan rotasi berkali-kali (misalnya metode $360^\\circ$, atau rangkaian pulsa panjang), sehingga galat kecil per pulsa menumpuk menjadi deviasi besar yang bisa diukur. Kita akan kembali ke ide ini di soal tantangan.\n"

